-- title:
--   Transport of level automorphism and supersingular point to j-chart
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $5 \le q$ and $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$; $\ell'$ is a prime with $\ell' \neq q$, $3 \le \ell'$ and $\ell' \nmid M'$. For a natural number $a$, [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH a M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) denotes the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/a^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/a)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $a$.
--
--   **Data over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which lies over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a non-unit of $A$ (`hA`). $W$ is a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` — the subfield of the Laurent series field over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$ generated by `jqModC` together with its $M'$-fold expansion `jqNModC _ M'` — and `hW` states that $W$ consists exactly of the members of `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, that is, of the places $w$ which are rational (the algebra map from the base field onto the residue field of $w$ is surjective), satisfy the predicate `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and for which $w.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of `jGeomGen` lies in `ssJSet q`, the set of those $j$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve with $j$-invariant $j$ has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $M'$ (adjoining the expansions `qExpand ℚ d jq` for $d \mid M'$) into `fieldBar q M'`, the function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $X_H$ of level $q^2M'$ for $H =$ `levelH q M'`. Next, $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` for $A$ from the base-changed field `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`: a valuation subring `integers` whose intersection with the constants is $A$, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the function field over the residue field with kernel the maximal ideal, compatibility of the residue map with the residue map of $A$ on constants, a scaling clause producing for each nonzero $f$ a constant $c$ with $c \cdot f$ integral and of nonzero residue, and a map on places preserving degrees and compatible with the push-forward of principal divisors. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, this element lies in $R_0$.`integers` and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **Admissible constants.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lies in $A$ (`hπ`). Write $A_0 := A \cap k_0$ for the pullback `A.comap (algebraMap k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`. The hypotheses on $A_0$ are: it is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), its maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$ (`hunif`), it is a henselian local ring (`hhens`), and its residue field is algebraically closed (`hres`); `hκ` requires every $a \in A$ to be congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to some element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   **Level $q\ell'$ over $k_0$.** $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell')$-th root of unity (`hξ`) and `hιξ` provides a ring homomorphism $k_0 \to \mathbb{C}$ carrying $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell')}$. The field $K$ is an intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(k_0)/k_0$ equal (`hK`) to the base change to $k_0$ of the function field of $X_H$ of level $(q\ell')^2M'$ with $H =$ `levelH (q*ℓ') M'`; $K$ carries an $A_0$-algebra structure compatible with the tower $A_0 \to k_0 \to K$. The element $j \in K$ is nonzero and its Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of `jq` (`hj`). Further, `hjK` asserts that `qExpand k₀ ℓ' (coeffEmb k₀ jq)` lies in $K$, and `hjC` that this element lies in `chartAlgFin A₀ K j`, the subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A_0[j]$.
--
--   **Moduli-theoretic input over $A_0$** (six hypotheses, their contents summarised here). `hℓ` requires that for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, variable change $C$ and quadruple $D = (x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$, if $D$ is an `IsLevelPStructure` for $W$ at $\ell'$ (both points on the curve, both annihilated by the $\ell'$-division polynomial `preΨ`, with both independence elements `indepElt` units) then `D.variableChange C` is one for $C \bullet W$. `hM` requires the analogous stability of `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` (a two-kernel condition when $p^k = 2$ and a cyclic-generator condition on $h$ otherwise) under variable change, the polynomial being transformed by `kernelVariableChangeDeg`. $\mathcal{G}$ is a family of relative group laws on the projective Weierstrass models over all $A_0$-algebras with unit discriminant, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`, each group law is computed by an evaluation on points) and with identity at the origin chart (`h𝒢O`). $\mathcal{T}$ is a `LevelTransport` for $\mathcal{G}$ at $q$, transporting raw Drinfeld pairs along variable changes and along $A_0$-algebra maps compatibly with the Drinfeld-basis condition, and `h𝒯` asserts that this transport is realised on the level of sections. `hVC` and `hCO` provide, for each variable change respectively each $A_0$-algebra map, a graded ring homomorphism between the graded rings of the projective models realising that variable change respectively that coefficient change, together with the inclusion of irrelevant ideals needed to induce a morphism of $\mathrm{Proj}$'s.
--
--   **The cyclotomic model.** $L_1$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for $\{q\ell'\}$, containing a primitive $q$-th root of unity $\zeta_1$ and a primitive $(q\ell')$-th root of unity $\xi_1$. $K_1$ is the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L_1)/L_1$ given by the same base-changed $X_H$ function field of level $(q\ell')^2M'$ and $H =$ `levelH (q*ℓ') M'` (`hK₁`). $A_1$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L_1$ such that $q$ lies in its maximal ideal (`hA₁q`) and $\zeta_1$ is in the image of $A_1$ (`hζA₁`); $K_1$ is an $A_1$-algebra compatibly with $A_1 \to L_1 \to K_1$. The element $j_1 \in K_1$ is nonzero with Laurent series the coefficientwise image of `jq` (`hj₁`), and $\varpi_1$ generates the maximal ideal of $A_1$ (`hϖ₁`). On the two-chart integral model `TwoChartIntegralModel A₁ K₁ j₁` (the pushout gluing the spectra of the chart algebras at $j_1$ and at $j_1^{-1}$), $z_1$ is a point and $\varpi z_1$ is the element of the stalk at $z_1$ obtained from $\varpi_1$ by pulling back along the structural morphism to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A_1$ and taking the germ at $z_1$ (`hϖz₁`); `hz₁` places $\varpi z_1$ in the maximal ideal of that stalk, so $z_1$ lies in the special fibre. The point $y_1$ of $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the finite chart algebra `chartAlgFin A₁ K₁ j₁` maps to $z_1$ (`hy₁`), and `hss₁` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel the prime of $y_1$, the image $\varphi(\mathtt{jChartFin})$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`; that is, $y_1$ is a supersingular point of the special fibre.
--
--   **Comparison of constants.** $\iota : L_1 \to k_0$ is a ring homomorphism with $\iota(\xi_1) = \xi$ (`hιξ₁`), mapping $A_1$ into $A$ (`hιA`), and such that any $x \in L_1$ with $\iota(x) \in A$ already comes from $A_1$ (`hιA'`); thus $A_1$ is the contraction of $A$ along $\iota$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Let $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ belong to $\Gamma_0(M')$ and to $\Gamma(\ell')$, let $\tau_1$ be an $L_1$-algebra automorphism of $K_1$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt L₁ (q*ℓ') ξ₁ (q*ℓ') ((q*ℓ')^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ') M') γ⁻¹ K₁ τ₁` — that is: for every weight $k$, every pair $f, g$ of modular forms of weight $k$ for [`CohCarrier.GammaH ((q*ℓ')^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ') M')`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133), every pair of integral power series $p_f, p_g$ which are the integral $q$-expansions of $f$ and of $g$ with the rational series of $p_g$ nonzero, every $x \in K_1$ whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of $\mathrm{intSeriesC}(p_f)/\mathrm{intSeriesC}(p_g)$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota' : L_1 \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota'(\xi_1) = e^{2\pi i/(q\ell')}$, the product of the coefficientwise image under $\iota'$ of $\tau_1 x$ with the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k \mathtt{conjElemN}\,(q\ell')\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k \mathtt{conjElemN}\,(q\ell')\,\gamma^{-1}$, where $\mathtt{conjElemN}\,m\,\delta$ is the determinant-one real matrix with entries $\delta_{00}, \delta_{01}/m, m\delta_{10}, \delta_{11}$ — and let `hpres₁` witness that $\tau_1$ maps `chartAlgFin A₁ K₁ j₁` into itself. Then there exist a ring homomorphism
--   $$c : \mathtt{chartAlgFin } A_1\, K_1\, j_1 \longrightarrow \mathtt{chartAlgFin } A_0\, K\, j,$$
--   an automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ over $k_0$, a proof that $\tau$ satisfies the same level-automorphism condition `IsLevelAutAt k₀ (q*ℓ') ξ (q*ℓ') ((q*ℓ')^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ') M') γ⁻¹ K τ` (with $(L_1, \xi_1, K_1, \tau_1)$ replaced by $(k_0, \xi, K, \tau)$), a proof `hpres` that $\tau$ maps `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` into itself, an element $s$ of the finite set $W$ of supersingular places, and a maximal ideal $y'$ of `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` containing the image of $\pi_0$, such that the following four assertions hold.
--
--   First, $c$ is coefficientwise application of $\iota$: for every $a$ in `chartAlgFin A₁ K₁ j₁`, the Laurent series over $k_0$ of $c(a)$ equals `coeffMap ι` applied to the Laurent series over $L_1$ of $a$.
--
--   Second, $c$ intertwines the two level automorphisms on the chart algebras: the restriction of $\tau$ to `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` composed with $c$ equals $c$ composed with the restriction of $\tau_1$ to `chartAlgFin A₁ K₁ j₁` (in the Lean, the composite in which the restriction of $\tau$ is applied after $c$ is equal to the composite in which $c$ is applied after the restriction of $\tau_1$).
--
--   Third, the contraction of $y'$ along $c$ is the prime of $y_1$: $\mathrm{comap}_c(y') = y_1$.
--
--   Fourth, $y'$ lies over the place $s$ in the following explicit sense. Let $g$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ lying in `modularFunctionFieldFull M'`, let `hgi` witness that its image in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` lies in $R_0$.`integers`, and assume that at every place $P$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ at which the image of `jq` has non-negative order, the image of $g$ also has non-negative order, and that the $R_0$-residue of $g$ lies in the valuation subring of $s$. Then for every witness `hgK` that `qExpand k₀ ℓ' (coeffEmb k₀ g)` lies in $K$, every witness `hgC` that this element lies in `chartAlgFin A₀ K j`, and every element of $k_0$ lying in $A$ (this binder again carries the name `c` in the Lean, shadowing the ring homomorphism) whose residue in $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$ equals the value $s.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the difference between the chart element determined by `qExpand k₀ ℓ' (coeffEmb k₀ g)` and the image of that constant under $A_0 \to$ `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` lies in $y'$.
--
--   This is the transport step which moves a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, and a supersingular closed point of the special fibre, from the canonical cyclotomic base $(A_1, L_1)$ with $L_1 = \mathbb{Q}(\zeta_{q\ell'})$ to an arbitrary admissible field of constants $k_0$ with its henselian discrete valuation ring $A_0 = A \cap k_0$, producing simultaneously the supersingular place $s$ of the level-$M'$ function field in characteristic $q$ over which the new point lies and the evaluation dictionary relating values of modular functions at that point to values at $s$. It is used by the three auxiliary lemmas on supersingular points of the two-chart integral model (`levelAut_sub_mem_of_isLevelAutAt_of_mem_Gamma_of_mem_ssJSet_twoChartIntegralModel`, `mem_asIdeal_of_coe_mem_nonunits_gaussValuationSubring_of_mem_ssJSet_twoChartIntegralModel` and `mem_of_forall_coeff_mem_maximalIdeal_of_mem_ssJSet_twoChartIntegralModel`), and rests on the existence and uniqueness of level automorphisms over $k_0$, the construction of a maximal ideal of the $j$-finite chart above $y_1$ and $\pi_0$, and the identification of the supersingular place underneath it.
